# Line endings are decided here rather than by each developer's core.autocrlf.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every text file in this repo is already stored with LF —
# 2,927 of them, verified against main before this was written, with zero CRLF
# and zero mixed. What was missing was anything *saying* so, and the gap showed
# up as merge conflicts rather than as bad bytes.
#
# Without a policy here, git falls back to each machine's core.autocrlf. On
# Windows that is normally `true`, which checks files out as CRLF and converts
# back to LF on commit — but it deliberately does NOT renormalise a file already
# stored with CRLF, so one file committed the wrong way stays wrong indefinitely.
# CHANGELOG.md was exactly that: stored CRLF for most of this repo's life until
# v1.402.0 happened to rewrite it whole. The next branch to touch it then
# conflicted on all 24,931 lines — two whole-file rewrites, no common ground —
# for a change that added seventy. Two open branches and a file everybody edits
# is all it takes, and CHANGELOG.md is a file EVERY change edits, by policy.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than bare `text=auto` is deliberate. `text=auto` alone would
# fix the stored bytes and leave Windows working trees on CRLF, which keeps the
# working tree and the index disagreeing — and that disagreement is what makes a
# byte-level edit (a script, or an agent writing a file) round-trip differently
# depending on who ran it. Everything here deploys to Linux; there is no reader
# that needs CRLF. Matching the working tree to the index makes what you see the
# thing that is committed.
#
# Applying this changes no committed content: `git add --renormalize .` stages
# nothing, because the index was already LF. It does rewrite text files in an
# existing Windows working tree on the next checkout, which is a no-op for git
# and silences the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning on every add.
#
# The sibling `triton` repo carries a narrower version of this file, listing the
# individual deploy scripts that must be LF to run on the VM. The rule below
# covers that case here too — see infra/configs/startup_script.sh.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files where a CRLF is not cosmetic but a failure: a
# shell script with CRLF line endings dies on Linux with `bad interpreter:
# /bin/bash^M`, and the Cloud Build YAML below is what deploys this app.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
cloudbuild.yaml text eol=lf
infra/**/*.yaml text eol=lf
infra/**/*.sh   text eol=lf

# Binary, never line-ending converted and never diffed as text. `text=auto`
# detects these correctly on its own; they are listed so that a misdetection on
# some future file cannot corrupt one silently.
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.png  binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.otf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
